1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,397
Frequency is the number you tune to.

2
00:00:04,397 --> 00:00:09,789
When you dial in 162.550 megahertz, you're not choosing a channel out of a list —

3
00:00:09,789 --> 00:00:15,182
you're selecting how many times per second a wave vibrates, and listening for the

4
00:00:15,182 --> 00:00:17,379
one wave in the air that matches.

5
00:00:17,379 --> 00:00:22,838
Formally: frequency is the number of cycles a periodic wave completes each second,

6
00:00:22,838 --> 00:00:26,633
measured in hertz — one hertz being one cycle per second.

7
00:00:26,633 --> 00:00:31,493
It's the coordinate the whole electromagnetic spectrum is laid out along.

8
00:00:31,943 --> 00:00:34,365
The everyday anchor is a metronome.

9
00:00:34,365 --> 00:00:38,102
Set it slow and you get one tick a second — one hertz.

10
00:00:38,102 --> 00:00:41,770
Speed it up and more ticks pack into the same second.

11
00:00:41,770 --> 00:00:46,338
Nothing about any single tick changed; only the counting rate did.

12
00:00:46,338 --> 00:00:50,422
Frequency is exactly that: a counting rate for wave cycles.

13
00:00:50,872 --> 00:00:55,566
Put two waves side by side over the same one second and you can see it.

14
00:00:55,566 --> 00:01:00,788
The top wave completes a handful of cycles; the bottom wave crams in many more.

15
00:01:00,788 --> 00:01:06,143
Same duration, same speed of travel — the only difference is how many cycles pass

16
00:01:06,143 --> 00:01:06,871
per second.

17
00:01:06,871 --> 00:01:11,829
And notice what higher frequency costs: each individual cycle gets shorter.

18
00:01:11,829 --> 00:01:16,523
More cycles per second means less time — and less distance — per cycle.

19
00:01:16,523 --> 00:01:21,283
That's the seed of the frequency–wavelength trade you'll use constantly.

20
00:01:21,733 --> 00:01:22,621
The numbers.

21
00:01:22,621 --> 00:01:28,686
Frequency is the inverse of period: if one cycle takes T seconds, the frequency is

22
00:01:28,686 --> 00:01:29,500
one over T.

23
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:35,492
A 100-megahertz FM signal repeats a hundred million times a second, so each cycle

24
00:01:35,492 --> 00:01:37,489
lasts just ten nanoseconds.

25
00:01:37,489 --> 00:01:42,963
Radio numbers run big, so they're scaled: kilohertz, thousands; megahertz,

26
00:01:42,963 --> 00:01:46,735
millions; gigahertz, billions of cycles per second.

27
00:01:46,735 --> 00:01:52,653
The unit honours Heinrich Hertz, who first generated and detected radio waves in

28
00:01:52,653 --> 00:01:53,392
the 1880s.

29
00:01:53,842 --> 00:01:55,439
Two consequences follow.

30
00:01:55,439 --> 00:02:00,361
First, because all radio waves travel at the speed of light, frequency and

31
00:02:00,361 --> 00:02:05,549
wavelength are locked together — doubling the frequency halves the wavelength.

32
00:02:05,549 --> 00:02:09,407
Second, frequency isn't just a dial setting; it's an axis.

33
00:02:09,407 --> 00:02:14,595
The frequency domain — what a spectrum analyzer or an FFT shows — plots energy

34
00:02:14,595 --> 00:02:19,717
against frequency, where a pure tone is a single spike and a modulated signal

35
00:02:19,717 --> 00:02:22,910
spreads into a band around its centre frequency.

36
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:28,717
Why you care: a transmitter and receiver must agree on frequency to within a small

37
00:02:28,717 --> 00:02:31,852
tolerance, and real oscillators don't cooperate.

38
00:02:31,852 --> 00:02:37,209
They drift with temperature and age, and their short-term jitter shows up as phase

39
00:02:37,209 --> 00:02:40,868
noise that smears the carrier and degrades demodulation.

40
00:02:40,868 --> 00:02:45,702
Motion shifts the apparent frequency too — Doppler shift — significant for

41
00:02:45,702 --> 00:02:47,596
satellites and fast vehicles.

42
00:02:47,596 --> 00:02:52,823
And beyond the physics there's law: the radio spectrum is divided into frequency

43
00:02:52,823 --> 00:02:57,069
bands, and regulators assign slices of each to specific services.

44
00:02:57,069 --> 00:03:01,381
A frequency is not just a number but a legal and physical context.

45
00:03:01,831 --> 00:03:06,997
In GopherTrunk, tuning sets the centre frequency the SDR's local oscillator mixes

46
00:03:06,997 --> 00:03:12,099
down toward baseband — the slice of air that lands in the spectrum and waterfall

47
00:03:12,099 --> 00:03:12,418
view.

48
00:03:12,418 --> 00:03:17,648
And because cheap tuners are off by a few parts per million, GopherTrunk applies a

49
00:03:17,648 --> 00:03:22,941
PPM correction and then continuously tracks residual frequency error with automatic

50
00:03:22,941 --> 00:03:25,684
frequency control, so symbols stay aligned.

51
00:03:25,684 --> 00:03:30,850
Getting the frequency right — and keeping it right — is the first requirement for

52
00:03:30,850 --> 00:03:31,551
any decode.

53
00:03:32,001 --> 00:03:37,715
So: frequency is a counting rate — cycles per second in hertz — it's the inverse of

54
00:03:37,715 --> 00:03:42,879
period, it fixes the wavelength, and holding it steady is job one for every

55
00:03:42,879 --> 00:03:43,498
receiver.

56
00:03:43,498 --> 00:03:45,357
Full write-up linked below.

